  <data name="ResamplingHelp.nearest"><value>Copies the nearest source pixel without smoothing; useful for pixel art and test patterns that need unchanged pixel values. It is fast, but enlarging photos can produce blocky edges and rough transitions.</value></data>
  <data name="ResamplingHelp.bilinear"><value>Blends nearby pixels for smooth transitions with fast processing. Suitable when speed matters, but it softens fine details and thin lines.</value></data>
  <data name="ResamplingHelp.bicubic"><value>Uses a cubic filter for a balance of sharpness and smooth tonal transitions in photos. It retains more detail than bilinear, but may create slight halos around high-contrast edges.</value></data>
  <data name="ResamplingHelp.lanczos"><value>Preserves fine detail well when mapping photos to the dithering grid, especially when reducing size. Choose it for sharper results, but watch for light or dark halos around high-contrast edges.</value></data>
  <data name="ResamplingHelp.mitchell"><value>Balances detail and smooth transitions with less edge ringing than Lanczos. Suitable for photos where halos are distracting, at the cost of slightly softer fine detail and slower processing.</value></data>
